The Nautilus DevOps team needs a time check pod created in a specific Kubernetes namespace for logging purposes. 
Initially, it's for testing, but it may be integrated into an existing cluster later. Here's what's required:


Create a pod called time-check in the devops namespace. The pod should contain a container named time-check, utilizing the busybox image with the latest tag (specify as busybox:latest).

Create a config map named time-config with the data TIME_FREQ=6 in the same namespace.

Configure the time-check container to execute the command: while true; do date; sleep $TIME_FREQ;done. 
Ensure the result is written /opt/security/time/time-check.log. Also, add an environmental variable TIME_FREQ in the container, fetching its value from the config map TIME_FREQ key.

Create a volume log-volume and mount it at /opt/security/time within the container.

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION

The devops namespace doesn't exist yet. Let's create it first, then apply the resources.

Step 1: Create the devops Namespace
bash
kubectl create namespace devops
Step 2: Apply the ConfigMap and Pod
bash
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: time-config
  namespace: devops
data:
  TIME_FREQ: "6"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: time-check
  namespace: devops
spec:
  containers:
  - name: time-check
    image: busybox:latest
    command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "while true; do date >> /opt/security/time/time-check.log; sleep \$TIME_FREQ; done"]
    env:
    - name: TIME_FREQ
      valueFrom:
        configMapKeyRef:
          name: time-config
          key: TIME_FREQ
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /opt/security/time
  volumes:
  - name: log-volume
    emptyDir: {}
EOF
Step 3: Verify
bash
# Confirm namespace exists
kubectl get ns devops

# Confirm resources
kubectl get configmap time-config -n devops
kubectl get pod time-check -n devops

# Wait a few seconds, then check the log is being written
sleep 15
kubectl exec -it time-check -n devops -- cat /opt/security/time/time-check.log

# Confirm the env var
kubectl exec -it time-check -n devops -- env | grep TIME_FREQ
You should now see:

time-config ConfigMap listed in devops

time-check pod in Running state

Log entries with timestamps appearing every 6 seconds

TIME_FREQ=6 in the container environment

If you'd prefer to make namespace creation idempotent (safe to re-run), use:

bash
kubectl create namespace devops --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
